exports[`the built deck on every example chapter > examples/presentation/01-slides/04-four-themes.md 1`] = `
"<section id="four-themes" class="slide slide-divider divider" data-kind="divider" data-line="1">
<h1 class="headline">Four themes</h1>
</section>
<section id="inclusion-and-wellbeing" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="3">
<h2 class="headline">Inclusion and <em>wellbeing</em>
</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="authored">
<p>Theme 1; straight to the heart of macromarketing thought.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide" class="slide slide-continuation" data-kind="continuation" data-line="9">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/digital-divide.svg" alt="ITU press release: detailing the globe's digital divides" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">ITU press release: detailing the globe's digital divides</span>
<span class="credit">ITU, Facts and Figures 2025</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>6 billion</strong> people are online, <strong>2.2 billion</strong> offline: Of those offline, 96% live in low- and middle-income countries <em>(ITU 2025)</em>
</li>
<li>5G coverage: <strong>84%</strong> of people in high-income countries vs. <strong>4%</strong> in low-income countries <em>(ibid.)</em>
</li>
<li>
<strong>54%</strong> of respondents living in low-income countries expect generative AI to leave them behind <em>(Edelman 2026)</em>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Sources: ITU, Facts and Figures 2025: itu.int; Edelman: edelman.com/trust/2026/trust-barometer.</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="does-the-system-serve-the-many-or-the-few" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="21">
<h2 class="headline">Does the system serve the many or the few?</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<ul>
<li>and who's measuring?</li>
</ul>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="energy-water-and-waste" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="25">
<h2 class="headline">Energy, water, and <em>waste</em>
</h2>
</section>
<section id="slide-2" class="slide slide-continuation" data-kind="continuation" data-line="27">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/energy-and-ai3.svg" alt="Global map of large data-centre clusters, 2024" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Global map of large data-centre clusters, 2024</span>
<span class="credit">IEA, Energy and AI (2025), Figure 1.13</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
<li>Engery consumption of data centres estimated to grow from 415 TWh in 2024 to <strong>~945 TWh by 2030</strong>; consuming an estimated ~1.5% of world electricity <em>(IEA 2025)</em>
</li>
<li>U.S.: <strong>4.4%</strong> of electricity today, projected to increase to <strong>6.7</strong> – <strong>12%</strong> by 2028 <em>(LBNL 2024)</em>
</li>
<li>Water consumption: <strong>17.5 bn gallons</strong> direct use in the U.S. (2023), set to increase x2 - x4 by 2028 <em>(LBNL)</em>; one facility in Georgia alone consumes <strong>~10% of its county's water</strong> supply <em>(Lincoln Institute 2026)</em>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Sources: IEA, Energy and AI — iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai · LBNL — eta.lbl.gov/publications/2024-united-states-data-center · Lincoln Institute — lincolninst.edu · water context: spectrum.ieee.org/ai-water-usage</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="slide-3" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="39">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/ewaste-monitor.svg" alt="UN press release: e-waste rising five times faster than documented recycling" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">UN press release: e-waste rising five times faster than documented recycling</span>
<span class="credit">Global E-waste Monitor 2024 — ewastemonitor.info</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>62 million tonnes</strong> of e-waste in 2022, estimated to grow to <strong>82 Mt</strong> by 2030; only <strong>22.3%</strong> are currently formally recycled <em>(GEM 2024)</em>
</li>
<li>Waste contains materials worth an estimated <strong>$91 billion</strong> (incl. <strong>~$15 billion of actual gold</strong>), of which $19bn currently recovered <em>(ibid.)</em>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Sources: Global E-waste Monitor 2024 (ITU/UNITAR): ewastemonitor.info.</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="do-big-tech-firms-really-have-the-social-licence-to-build-what-they-re-building" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="48">
<h2 class="headline">Do <em>big tech</em> firms really have the <em>social</em> licence to build what they're building?</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<ul>
<li>and who's giving them permission?<sup class="footnote-reference">licence</sup>
</li>
</ul>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="concentration-and-power" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="54">
<h2 class="headline">Concentration and <em>power</em>
</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="authored">
<p>Theme 3</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide-4" class="slide slide-continuation" data-kind="continuation" data-line="60">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/rbc2.svg" alt="Cumulative weight of the 10 largest S&amp;P 500 companies, 1990–2025" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Cumulative weight of the 10 largest S&amp;P 500 companies, 1990–2025</span>
<span class="credit">RBC Wealth Management / FactSet, January 2026</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
<li>The ten largest S&amp;P 500 firms: A record <strong>40+%</strong> of the index, twice their 2015 weight <em>(RBC, 2026)</em>
</li>
<li>Three cloud provider (AWS, Azure, Google) hold <strong>~59%</strong> of hyperscale capacity <em>(Synergy Research)</em>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Sources: RBC Wealth Management / FactSet — rbcwealthmanagement.com/en-us/insights/the-great-narrowing-sp-500-concentration · capacity share: Synergy Research, via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_center</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="slide-5" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="71">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/fable.svg" alt="Statement by Anthropic, published two days ago" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Statement by Anthropic, published two days ago</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Anthropic (June 2026): anthropic.com.</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="slide-6" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="77">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/ai-bots.svg" alt="Key findings from the 2026 Bad Bot Report: bots now drive 53% of web traffic — and AI agents are a new category of internet participant" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Key findings from the 2026 Bad Bot Report: bots now drive 53% of web traffic — and AI agents are a new category of internet participant</span>
<span class="credit">Thales / Imperva, 2026 Bad Bot Report</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Source: Thales / Imperva — imperva.com/blog/bad-bot-report-2026-bots-agentic-age</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="what-happens-when-this-hits-markets" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="83">
<h2 class="headline">What happens when this hits <em>markets</em>?</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="authored">
<p>The question for Theme 3</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="agents-as-market-actors" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="89">
<h2 class="headline">Agents as market actors</h2>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Sources: Thales/Imperva — imperva.com · McKinsey &amp; Gartner via nshift.com · Morgan Stanley via commercetools.com · protocols &amp; checkout: opascope.com</p>
</footer>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<ul>
<li>2025: <strong>53%</strong> of web traffic was automated; the human share keeps falling <em>(Thales/Imperva, 2026)</em>
</li>
<li>Projections:<sup class="footnote-reference">projections</sup> <strong>$3–5 trillion</strong> of retail by 2030 <em>(McKinsey)</em>; <strong>$15 trillion</strong> of B2B by 2028 <em>(Gartner)</em>; <strong>~half of online shoppers</strong> via agents by 2030 <em>(Morgan Stanley)</em>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
<em>
<strong>When markets were built for humans, what is a marketing system when the "consumer" is an algorithm? And the protocols underlying all of this belong to a few firms?</strong>
</em>
</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="information-and-trust" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="102">
<h2 class="headline">Information and <em>trust</em>
</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="authored">
<p>Theme 2</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide-7" class="slide slide-continuation" data-kind="continuation" data-line="108">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/graphite.svg" alt="Graphite's crossover chart: AI-generated articles surpassed human-written content in November 2024" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Graphite's crossover chart: AI-generated articles surpassed human-written content in November 2024</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
<li>The majority of new articles published on the web are now estimated to be AI-generated <em>(Graphite 2025)</em>
</li>
<li>
<strong>Related:</strong> In a different study, <strong>up to 45%</strong> of open-ended online-survey responses are estimated to be copied from LLM output <em>(Nature 2026)</em>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Source: Graphite: graphite.io/five-percent/more-articles-are-now-created-by-ai-than-humans; Nature: nature.com/articles/d41586-026-01726-y.</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="slide-8" class="slide slide-continuation" data-kind="continuation" data-line="119">
<div class="face">
<ul>
<li>
<strong>7 in 10</strong> people hesitant to trust those with different values or source of information <em>(Edelman 2026)</em>
</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Misinformation (<strong>50%</strong>) and generative AI (<strong>37%</strong>) among the top five things that shape trust <em>(ibid.)</em>
</li>
<li>Only <strong>32%</strong> of respondents believe the next generation will be better off <em>(ibid.)</em>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="credit">Source: 2026 Edelman Trust Barometer: edelman.com/trust/2026/trust-barometer.</p>
</footer>
</section>
<section id="what-happens-to-exchange-when-information-is-infinite-and-trust-a-scarcity" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="129">
