exports[`the built deck on every example chapter > examples/manuscript/01-manuscript/01-introduction.md 1`] = `
"<section id="introduction" class="slide slide-title" data-kind="title" data-line="1">
<h1 class="headline">Introduction</h1>
<footer class="slide-foot">
<p class="footnote">
<span class="label">projects</span>The applications are published on GitHub.</p>
</footer>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>The year 1977 marked a turning point when computers began reaching non-expert users. Three machines shifted computing from hobbyist niche to mainstream, with the Apple II widely regarded as the most refined of the trio. Unusual for its time, the machine was launched with an elaborate marketing campaign positioning it as "an extraordinary device for ordinary people" <span class="citation">[@hey2015computing]</span>. Marketing for the Apple II signalled a shift that would define the decades to come: computing as a <em>consumer</em> product. Over the past decades, successive waves of innovation---the Internet, mobile computing, ubiquitous cloud services, and most recently, AI assistants based on large language models---have progressively expanded both access to and the utility of computing. Adoption rates have accelerated with each wave; ChatGPT for example, perhaps the most prominent of the current generation of AI assistants, grew faster at launch than almost any consumer technology in history <span class="citation">[@milmo2023chatgpt; @epochai2025chatgpt]</span>.</p>
<p>Today, personal computing permeates every aspect of life. We have become reliant on these technologies for tasks ranging from the mundane, such as coordinating with friends, to those requiring the orchestration of vast computational resources, including real-time language translation or on-demand video creation. For many of us, it is now difficult, if not impossible, to imagine a life without the services enabled by modern computing devices.</p>
<p>Yet whilst we <em>use</em> these applications constantly, an intriguing question emerges: can non-expert users, lacking formal training, now also <em>develop</em> them? Perhaps the most significant recent development in this regard is <em>example document</em>---a term coined by Andrej Karpathy <span class="citation">[@karpathy2025vibe]</span> to describe a mode of software development in which users without programming backgrounds create working software applications through conversing with an AI assistant: the user articulates an intention and the AI proposes and executes an implementation. Together, they refine the result through iterative dialogue until the software functions as intended. Whether this represents a comparable moment to the emergence of personal computing---an extraordinary skill now accessible to ordinary people---is the question this paper explores.</p>
<p>At first glance, example document seems genuinely exciting. The journalist Lauren Goode, for instance, spent two days at a software company building working features using the approach <span class="citation">[@goode2025notion]</span>. Yet whilst such examples demonstrate what is possible, they may also fuel inflated expectations among those unfamiliar with the practice. Managers seeking productivity gains, for example, may assume that example document requires nothing more than issuing instructions for the AI to execute.</p>
<p>To test this assumption, I immersed myself in example document a series of applications. My aim was to address a central question in the current discourse: <em>to what extent can non-experts develop software ready for real-world use?</em> Three objectives guided my exploration:</p>
<ol>
<li>To evaluate the current state of example document for different audiences, including non-experts</li>
<li>To assess whether it is possible to vibe code transparently in ways that support both reproducibility and appropriate attribution of the AI's role</li>
<li>To articulate an initial framework of principles for non-experts approaching example document</li>
</ol>
<p>I find that while example document significantly lowers the barrier to software development, effective use still requires structured iteration and critical evaluation of AI outputs---or, as Eisenhower <a href="https://quoteinvestigator.com/2017/11/18/planning/">reportedly</a> put it, "Plans are worthless, but planning is everything."</p>
<p>The remainder of this manuscript proceeds as follows. I first review the concept of example document and discuss the current state of the art. I then describe how my practice-based approach evolved over three months, yielding multiple projects now publicly available on GitHub.<sup class="footnote-reference">projects</sup> Drawing on both, I articulate an initial example document framework of principles for non-experts. I conclude with an outline of future research opportunities.</p>
</aside>
</section>"
`;

exports[`the built deck on every example chapter > examples/manuscript/01-manuscript/02-the-current-state-of-the-art.md 1`] = `
"<section id="the-current-state-of-the-art" class="slide slide-title" data-kind="title" data-line="1">
<h1 class="headline">The current state-of-the-art</h1>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Vibe coding suggests that non-experts can now develop working software, and the practice has rapidly gained attention. For example, a recent survey identifies over 1,000 sources on the topic <span class="citation">[@ge2025survey]</span>, and <em>Collins Dictionary</em> named it <em>Word of the Year</em> <span class="citation">[@collins2025woty]</span>. Yet popularity alone does not answer whether the practice delivers on its promise. This section examines that question, first reviewing the opportunities and challenges, followed by a summary of current development frameworks.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="opportunities-and-challenges" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="5">
<h2 class="headline">Opportunities and challenges</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Without human review or comprehension of what the AI coding assistant produces, example document is essentially the opposite of how an expert software developer would use an AI assistant. Instead of a full review of what the assistant produces, example document accepts everything without review. If an error appears, the user hands the error message to the AI assistant for interpretation. Both constitute extreme points on a continuum of AI-assisted software development (Figure <a href="#fig:dev-spectrum">1</a>).</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="9">
<div class="face">
<figure id="fig:dev-spectrum" class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/approach-spectrum.svg" alt="The Al-Assisted Development Continuum." />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">The Al-Assisted Development Continuum.</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>
<section id="slide-2" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="11">
<div class="face">
<figure id="fig:dev-taxonomy" class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/arxiv-taxonomy.svg" alt="Development Models Taxonomy [adapted from @ge2025survey]." />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Development Models Taxonomy <span class="citation">[adapted from @ge2025survey]</span>.</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Moreover, example document differs from related developments such as <em>agentic coding</em>, in which autonomous software agents work independently towards a specified goal <span class="citation">[@sapkota2025agentic]</span>. By contrast, example document offers a distinctly human-in-the-loop model of software development, requiring continuous human <em>judgement</em> and <em>direction</em> rather than delegating to autonomous software agents.</p>
<p>Vibe coding's human-in-the-loop model appeals because it <em>appears</em> to offer three things traditional software development does not: accessibility, speed, and flow. First, it lowers the barrier of creating software; those without programming backgrounds can articulate intent in natural language rather than learning syntax. Second, it promises rapid iteration; ideas can become working prototypes within hours rather than weeks. Third, practitioners report a distinctive experience of <em>collaborative</em> creation with AI, characterised by trust and what one study describes as "flow" <span class="citation">[@pimenova2025vibrations]</span>. Whether these benefits materialise in practice remains uncertain. This tension is captured by <span class="citation">[@fawzy2025grey]</span>, whose analysis:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"reveals a speed-quality trade-off paradox, where vibe coders are motivated by speed and accessibility, often experiencing rapid 'instant success and flow,' yet most perceive the resulting code as fast but flawed" (p. 1).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Beyond the speed-quality tension, software security presents an even more fundamental challenge <span class="citation">[@tihanyi2024secure]</span>. LLM-based coding assistants can both miss genuine vulnerabilities and report false ones <span class="citation">[@basic2024vulnerabilities]</span>. Vulnerabilities have also been shown to compound with subsequent iterations <span class="citation">[@shukla2025degradation]</span>. Indeed, one study puts the percentage of working code generated by AI that can also be considered as secure at around 10% <span class="citation">[@zhao2025safe]</span>.</p>
<p>Following from these technical concerns, the <em>subjective</em> experience of example document warrants further attention. While the aforementioned qualitative study by <span class="citation">[@pimenova2025vibrations]</span> identifies trust, flow, and collaborative creation as central factors shaping perceptions of example document amongst developers, systematic accounts from non-expert audiences remain scarce. That said, reports begin to emerge suggesting that non-experts can create functional applications using natural language alone <span class="citation">[@techxplore2025enables]</span>, and Lauren Goode's <span class="citation">[@goode2025notion]</span> aforementioned account demonstrates this promise under the condition of expert supervision.</p>
<p>Given these challenges, what guidance currently exists for those wishing to vibe code?</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="guidance-and-approaches" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="25">
<h2 class="headline">Guidance and approaches</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Existing example document frameworks and best-practice summaries target different audiences (see Table <a href="#tab:approaches">1</a>). Those aimed at experts include <em>go-vibe</em> <span class="citation">[@govibe2025methodology]</span>, the <em>GitHub Spec Kit</em> <span class="citation">[@github2025speckit]</span>, <em>context engineering</em> <span class="citation">[@mugrage2025context]</span>, and the <em>CLEAR</em> framework for structured prompting <span class="citation">[@coderide2025clear]</span>. They have in common that they reflect an evolving recognition that systematic context management differs from the ad-hoc, intuition-led prompting with which example document is typically associated. Security-focused guidance has also emerged: the <em>Cloud Security Alliance</em>'s <em>Secure Example Document Guide</em> <span class="citation">[@csa2025secure]</span> explicitly targets "citizen developers" <span class="citation">[@csa2025secure]</span>, that is, non-technical audiences building applications with AI assistance.</p>
<table id="tab:approaches">
<caption>AI-assisted Development Frameworks.</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Approach</th>
<th>Focus</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>go-vibe</td>
<td>Documentation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>GitHub Spec Kit</td>
<td>Specifications</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Context Engineering</td>
<td>Context</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CLEAR Framework</td>
<td>Prompts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CSA Secure Example Document</td>
<td>Security</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Then there are practitioner views, such as specification-driven development <span class="citation">[@vemula2025specdriven]</span>, test-driven development <span class="citation">[e.g., @punnen2025prompts]</span>
</p>
<p>Several themes emerge from reviewing these approaches. First, the importance of providing structured context over vague prompting. Second, the need to verify and review, irrespective of whether the process is automated or human-led. Finally, an emerging tension between developer-centric frameworks that assume technical depth and those aimed at "citizen developers" without technical background---with the latter aligning with the common yet vague promise of "democratising access" to a new technology. Within this tension, the distinction between vibe and agentic coding <span class="citation">[@sapkota2025agentic]</span> is instructive. It positions example document as a human-in-the-loop approach suited to "early-stage prototyping and education"---perhaps the clearest framing to which non-technical creators can relate.</p>
<p>Consequently, different approaches to example document are more or less suitable for different audiences, which are best thought of as sitting on a continuum ranging from <em>expert</em> users (i.e., software developers) to <em>non-technical</em> users (i.e., "citizen developers"). As neither of those labels describes my own practice, I will first articulate the distinction between different audiences for example document, before detailing my own exploration of this practice.</p>
</aside>
</section>"
`;

exports[`the built deck on every example chapter > examples/manuscript/01-manuscript/03-audiences.md 1`] = `
"<section id="audiences" class="slide slide-title" data-kind="title" data-line="1">
<h1 class="headline">Audiences</h1>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Two broad groups can be distinguished: those using example document (which I refer to as <em>creators</em>) and those assessing decisions around its adoption (<em>assessors</em>). In my exploration, I focus on creators, which means that frameworks for those having to assess whether (and how) example document should be adopted in an organisation are outside the scope of my investigation; an example of a framework for assessors is ISACA's risk-based assessment framework <span class="citation">[@isaca2025vibecoding]</span> that targets organisational governance rather than individual practice, thus helping assessors categorise use cases by risk level to determine the appropriate level of oversight required.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="experts-non-experts-and-non-technical-users" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="5">
<h2 class="headline">Experts, non-experts, and non-technical users</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Creators can be further separated into groups based on purpose and scope:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Expert users</strong> develop software professionally, work within teams on large codebases and often complex deployment pipelines</li>
<li>
<strong>Non-expert users</strong> code for personal or ancillary purposes (e.g., for automation and data analysis), and tend to work alone on small scripts</li>
<li>
<strong>Non-technical users</strong> have no coding experience and thus rely entirely on natural language</li>
</ol>
<p>In practical terms, these three groups represent points on a continuum rather than discrete categories. The distinction between experts and the other two groups is fairly straightforward; the boundary between non-experts and non-technical users is fuzzier.</p>
<p>Since objective markers such as training or education typically do not apply, a more useful distinction is comfort with conventional developer tools. Non-expert users are typically familiar with version control (e.g., <em>Git</em>), a code editor, or the command line, even if they use these tools in limited ways. Non-technical users would be unfamiliar with such tools and rely entirely on natural language interfaces. In short, while both groups lack formal training, they differ in whether they have accumulated working knowledge through personal projects.</p>
<p>This distinction in terms of familiarity matters as non-expert and non-technical creators both should possess what I consider essential prerequisites for successful example document: an openness to coding to solve problems combined with an interest in learning new skills. An apt label to express this attitude is <em>enthusiastic dilettante</em>: an openness and dedication to coding, yet without the experience and/or understanding of how to do it at scale.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="enthusiastic-dilettantes" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="19">
<h2 class="headline">Enthusiastic dilettantes</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Although the modern meaning of the word "dilettante" describes a <em>dabbler</em> showing "a superficial interest," I personally carry the label with pride. This is because of its previous meaning "an admirer or lover of the arts" <span class="citation">[@merriamwebster2025dilettante]</span>. It is that meaning which describes the <em>attitude</em> necessary for making example document a success: an admirer of the art of coding. Moreover, as I will elaborate further below, the label aligns with my framing of AI assistants as <em>professional dilettantes</em>: breathtakingly and often surprisingly capable in breadth, yet prone to errors experienced developers would catch immediately, and on occasions showing behaviours they would find difficult to tolerate.</p>
<p>This is the framing in which I have pursued my personal example document journey, which I describe in the next section.</p>
</aside>
</section>"
`;

exports[`the built deck on every example chapter > examples/manuscript/01-manuscript/04-a-practice-led-exploration.md 1`] = `
"<section id="a-practice-led-exploration" class="slide slide-title" data-kind="title" data-line="1">
<h1 class="headline">A practice-led exploration</h1>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>I use coding predominantly to support my teaching and research work. For each new task, I set myself the goal of learning something new---whether it's a practice, concept, or tool. Past projects include workflow optimisations and what Bruce Sterling refers to as <em>data wrangling</em> tasks <span class="citation">[@sterling2005shaping]</span>. Concrete outcomes were, for example, the programmatic use of single-sourcing for teaching materials <span class="citation">[@reppel2019advising]</span> and an analysis of social media data <span class="citation">[@cappellini2019shouting]</span>.</p>
<p>Given my own positioning as an enthusiastic dilettante, I embraced the <em>vibe</em> of example document. Approaching example document intuitively, I made the conscious decision not to begin with a comprehensive review of the literature. Instead of drawing on emerging best practice, I was guided by my own experiences, such as past failures and the challenges I have thus far been unable to overcome. In other words, my exploration followed a challenge-led approach.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="challenge-led-approach" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="7">
<h2 class="headline">Challenge-led approach</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Specifically, I set myself the goal of example document three distinct applications to benefit my future work. To these, I subsequently added a fourth application. The first three have in common that they address a challenge I had previously been unable to resolve: as an academic, I am not permitted to upload content of a potentially sensitive nature to cloud-based large language models. This includes interview transcripts (if not explicitly covered by an ethics application and highlighted to participants), and academic publications, such as digital copies of books and journal articles typically not licensed to be uploaded to cloud-based services.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="application-development" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="11">
<h2 class="headline">Application development</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>The four applications were: a local <em>Retrieval-Augmented Generation</em> (RAG) system that lets me ask questions about documents while keeping them local and private; a <em>Persona</em> generator for creating user personas from qualitative data such as interview transcripts; and a <em>qualitative coding tool</em>. All three applications can run entirely with local large language models, though the <em>Persona</em> and <em>qualitative coding tool</em> also support cloud-based models. I then applied the approach that had emerged during development of initial versions of these three applications to a fourth one. The challenge for here was to develop a user-centric application for non-technical audiences, rather than a tool for my personal use.</p>
<p>The four applications were developed over a period of three months with regular example document sessions during the last two months (see Figure <a href="#fig:velocity-timeline">3</a>). Within this period, I created eight projects across four lineages, with 682K+ lines of code, over 500 documented features, and more than 150 decisions around architecture.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="17">
<div class="face">
<figure id="fig:velocity-timeline" class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/velocity-timeline.svg" alt="Development Timeline of Different Applications. (Height indicates number of commits.)" />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Development Timeline of Different Applications. (Height indicates number of commits.)</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Documentation emerged organically during development. For example, the first version of the RAG application began as an entirely vibe coded experiment (now archived on GitHub as <em>raggedZero</em>) and was quickly abandoned. The documentation developed for this initial experiment then formed the basis for a second iteration (archived as <em>ragged</em>) where the AI coding assistant was essentially left to their own devices and developed a very large code base of increasing complexity. This iteration was also abandoned, but led to important insights in terms of what should be recorded during development. This was then applied to a third iteration (<em>ragd</em>) that constitutes the first usable version of the application. The <em>Persona</em> and <em>qualitative coding</em> applications were developed in a similar way (building on <em>PersonaZero</em> and <em>eval</em> respectively). The fourth application, <em>itemdeck.app</em>, was developed using the structure and process that emerged during development of the other three applications.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide-2" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="21">
<div class="face">
<figure id="fig:spec-stack" class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/specification-stack.svg" alt="Example Document Spec Stack." />
<figcaption>
<span class="caption">Example Document Spec Stack.</span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>
<section id="results" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="23">
<h2 class="headline">Results</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="generated">
<p>Results of my exploration inform a framework of principles presented in the next section.</p>
</aside>
</section>"
`;

