<section id="slide-7" class="slide slide-continuation" data-kind="continuation" data-line="28">
<div class="face">
<blockquote>
<p>"Give a small boy a hammer, and he will find that everything he encounters needs pounding."
--Abraham Kaplan <em>(1964)</em>
</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<aside class="notes" data-source="authored">
<p>Abraham Kaplan calls "The Law of the Instrument" <span class="citation">[@Kaplan:1964tb, p. 28]</span> ...</p>
<p>... which suggests that we have a tendency to approach tasks using instruments we are familiar with and, probably more importantly, the related temptation to avoid what cannot be addressed that way <span class="citation">[cf. @Kagan:2003ud]</span>. Kaplan illustrates it with the quote you see behind me on the screen.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="slide-8" class="slide slide-image" data-kind="image" data-line="39">
<div class="face">
<figure class="full-bleed">
<img src="assets/command-line.svg" />
</figure>
</div>
</section>
<section id="planning-planning-planning" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="41">
<h2 class="headline">Planning, planning, planning!</h2>
<aside class="notes" data-source="authored">
<p>As Eisenhower reportedly put it: "Plans are worthless, but planning is everything." Vibe coding enables a "review &amp; resubmit" mentality—use it.</p>
</aside>
</section>
<section id="practice-practice-practice" class="slide slide-section" data-kind="section" data-line="49">
